const e=[{id:"l-liberties",moduleId:"m03-constitutional",title:"Fundamental liberties, and how a restriction is tested",minutes:25,summary:"The rights in Part II of the Constitution, the closed list of reasons Parliament may cut them down, and an argument the courts have not settled.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-art10-01"],reading:[{bookId:"faruqi-our-constitution",where:"the chapters on fundamental liberties"},{bookId:"faruqi-document-of-destiny",where:"for the longer treatment once the outline is secure"}],source:"Federal Constitution Arts 4(1), 5, 8, 10, 11; PP v Pung Chen Choon [1994] 1 MLJ 566 (FC); Sivarasa Rasiah v Badan Peguam Malaysia [2010] 2 MLJ 333 (FC); Muhammad Hilman bin Idham v Kerajaan Malaysia [2011] 6 MLJ 507 (CA).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"This is a live area. Check all three citations in a report you can hold, and check whether any later Federal Court decision has resolved the tension between Pung Chen Choon and Sivarasa — if one has, the shape of the answer changes.",plate:"rostrum",plateCaption:"A speaker, an audience, and the cordon. The argument is almost never about whether the right exists.",readMinutes:11,sections:[{id:"sec-l-liberties-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A law that punishes an ordinary complaint is where constitutional argument actually starts.",h:"Why this matters",body:[{t:"p",text:"Aminah posts a photograph of a broken road and asks why nobody has fixed it. A week later she is told that the post breaks the law. Can a law really say that?"},{t:"p",text:"The Federal Constitution gives Aminah a right to speak, and it also lets Parliament cut that right down. Almost every real argument in this area is about the cutting down rather than about the right itself."}]},{id:"sec-l-liberties-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Part II sets out the rights; the fight is almost never about whether the right exists.",h:"The rights themselves",body:[{t:"p",text:"Constitutional argument in Malaysia rarely fails because nobody could find the relevant right. It fails because the restriction survives, and the restriction is where the analytical work actually sits."},{t:"p",text:"Part II of the Federal Constitution sets out the basic rights of a person in Malaysia. Lawyers call them the **fundamental liberties**, and the four you will meet most often are these."},{t:"list",items:["Article 5 — liberty of the person, meaning that your freedom may be taken away only in a way the law allows.","Article 8 — equality before the law, and the equal protection of the law for everybody.","Article 10 — freedom of speech, freedom to meet together, and freedom to form groups.","Article 11 — freedom of religion, and the right to profess and practise it."]},{t:"p",text:"These are not simply fine words on a page. Article 4(1) makes the Constitution the highest law in the country, which is what turns the list into something a court can enforce."},{t:"p",text:"Article 4(1) makes the Constitution the supreme law of the Federation, so any law that is inconsistent with it is void to the extent of that inconsistency."},{t:"p",text:"*Void* means of no legal effect at all, from the very beginning. Notice the last few words as well: the law is void only so far as it clashes, and the rest of the Act keeps working."}]},{id:"sec-l-liberties-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Article 10(1)(a) gives citizens free speech, and Article 10(2)(a) lets Parliament cut it down on listed grounds.",h:"A right, and the power to cut it down",body:[{t:"p",text:"Aminah is a citizen, and her post criticises the way a state agency spends public money, so start with the right she is relying on."},{t:"rule",text:"Article 10(1)(a): every citizen has the right to freedom of speech and expression.",source:"Federal Constitution, Art 10(1)(a)"},{t:"caution",text:"Look hard at the word **citizen**, which is doing a great deal of work in that sentence. Article 10(1) belongs to citizens alone, while Article 5 is wider because it speaks of *persons* and so covers anybody in the country. If the person you are helping is not a citizen, Article 10 will not help them, and that single word ends some arguments before they begin."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Aminah is a citizen, and Article 10(1)(a) gives her the right to speak. Before you read on — can a law still make her post an offence?",options:["No — the Constitution gives her the right, so a law against it is void","Yes, but only for the reasons the Constitution itself lists","Yes, for any reason Parliament thinks is a good one"],answer:1,reveal:"The second one. If you picked the first, that is what the word *right* normally means, and it is a fair instinct to have. This Constitution does not work that way. It hands the right over in one Article and lets Parliament cut it down in the next. The third answer is the other half of the trap. Parliament's reasons are not free either. They have to come off a list, and the list is in Article 10(2)."},{t:"p",text:"The right is not the end of the story, because Parliament may pass a law that restricts it — though not for any reason it happens to like."},{t:"rule",text:"Article 10(2)(a): Parliament may by law impose on the right conferred by 10(1)(a) such restrictions as it deems necessary or expedient in the interest of the security of the Federation, friendly relations with other countries, public order or morality, and restrictions designed to protect the privileges of Parliament or to provide against contempt of court, defamation, or incitement to any offence.",source:"Federal Constitution, Art 10(2)(a)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: Parliament may cut the right down, but only for the reasons that Article names."},{t:"p",text:"Those grounds are the security of the Federation, friendly relations with other countries, public order, and morality. Parliament may also restrict speech to protect its own privileges, and to guard against contempt of court, defamation, or encouraging somebody to commit an offence."},{t:"p",text:"**Defamation** means harming a person's reputation by what you say or write. **Contempt of court** covers behaviour that obstructs a court or undermines its authority."},{t:"p",text:"That list is closed, and nothing may be added to it by a court or by Parliament. If a law restricts speech for some reason outside the list, Article 10(2)(a) never permitted it in the first place."},{t:"p",text:"This gives you a strong opening argument that needs no theory at all. Ask whether the restriction fits one of the named grounds, and if it does not, the law is simply not authorised."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"The question to ask before any theory",caption:"The list in Article 10(2) is closed. A restriction that fits none of the named grounds fails on that alone, and the argument never has to reach proportionality.",alt:"A question — does the restriction fit a ground named in Article 10(2)? — branching two ways. Yes: the argument moves on to how hard a judge may look at necessity. No: the restriction fails, because nothing may be added to the list by a court or by Parliament.",question:"Does the restriction fit a ground named in Art 10(2)?",branches:[{cond:"yes",label:"Move to how hard a judge may look",note:"deference, or proportionality"},{cond:"no",label:"It fails on that alone",note:"the list is closed; nothing may be added"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-liberties-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"How hard a court may look behind a restriction is unsettled — Pung Chen Choon and Sivarasa both stand.",h:"How hard may a judge look?",body:[{t:"p",text:"Now suppose the restriction does fit one of those grounds — may a judge then go on and ask whether it was really necessary? Two lines of Malaysian authority pull in different directions, and it is worth knowing both rather than picking the one you prefer."},{t:"rule",text:"PP v Pung Chen Choon: the words “deems necessary or expedient” leave the necessity of a restriction largely to Parliament; the court's role is to ask whether the restriction falls within a permitted head.",source:"PP v Pung Chen Choon [1994] 1 MLJ 566 (FC)"},{t:"rule",text:"Sivarasa Rasiah: fundamental liberties are to be read generously, restrictions must be reasonable, and proportionality is imported through Art 8(1).",source:"Sivarasa Rasiah v Badan Peguam Malaysia [2010] 2 MLJ 333 (FC)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: on the first view the court checks only that the restriction fits one of the listed grounds. On the second it goes further and asks whether the restriction was reasonable."},{t:"p",text:"**Proportionality** is a formal name for a plain question: is the harm the law does to the right worse than the good the law achieves?"},{t:"p",text:"Muhammad Hilman is the case where that second approach did real work. The Court of Appeal struck down a rule limiting the political speech of students, holding the restriction unreasonable, which shows the reasonableness line is more than talk."},{t:"compare",title:"Two lines of authority, both still standing",left:{h:"PP v Pung Chen Choon",items:["“Deems necessary or expedient” leaves necessity largely to Parliament.","The court asks whether the restriction fits a ground that Article 10(2) names.","It does not weigh whether the restriction was really needed.","The check is real, but it is a check on fit rather than on degree."]},right:{h:"Sivarasa Rasiah",items:["Fundamental liberties are to be read generously.","A restriction must be reasonable, not merely listed.","Proportionality enters through Article 8(1).","Muhammad Hilman is where this approach did real work, striking down a rule limiting student political speech."]}},{t:"caution",text:"Do not write that Sivarasa overruled Pung Chen Choon, because it did not, and both decisions still stand with the tension between them unresolved. Hold both, argue that the question of *which ground* is for the court on either view, and keep reasonableness as your second argument rather than your only one."}]},{id:"sec-l-liberties-4",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Five steps: name the right, check the holder, find the restricting law, test the head, then test reasonableness.",h:"Building the argument, step by step",body:[{t:"list",items:["Name the right, and check that the person can actually hold it — for Article 10(1), that means being a citizen.","Name the law that restricts the right, and say exactly what it forbids.","Ask whether the restriction fits one of the grounds listed in Article 10(2), remembering that the question belongs to the court and not to Parliament.","Only then argue that the restriction is unreasonable or out of proportion.","State the result under Article 4(1): the law is void so far as it clashes, and not void entirely."]},{t:"p",text:"Step three carries most of the weight, and there is a reason for that. If Parliament could decide for itself that a law fitted a ground, Article 4(1) would have nothing left to bite on."},{t:"example",text:"An Act punishes “any statement likely to cause public disquiet about the conduct of the police”, and its long title says that it protects public order. But public order is about disorder — riots, fights, disturbance in the street. People feeling uneasy about the police is not disorder, so the label Parliament chose does not settle the question — the court decides that for itself."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"An Act restricts speech in order to protect the dignity of a government department. Where does the argument end?",options:["The law was never authorised — that reason is not on the Article 10(2) list","The court weighs the harm to speech against the good the law does","Parliament decided the reason was a good one, and that settles it"],answer:0,why:"The list in Article 10(2)(a) is closed. The dignity of a department is not on it, so the restriction was never permitted and there is nothing further to weigh. The trap is the second answer. Reasonableness is a real argument, but reaching for it here walks past the simpler one that wins outright."},{t:"caution",text:"What a law *does* matters more than how it is written. A law that never mentions speech can still restrict speech, and the court looks at the effect rather than at the drafting."}]},{id:"sec-l-liberties-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"No cards here yet, so the reading carries the detail and the problem question is the test.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are no flashcards for this module yet, so the reading list is where the detail lives and the problem question is the test."},{t:"p",text:"It gives you a journalist charged under an invented provision, and a prosecutor who argues that the word “deems” hands the whole question to Parliament. A good answer meets that argument head on instead of reciting the right."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qlb1",q:"Who holds the right in Article 10(1)(a)?",options:["Every person in the Federation","Every natural person","Citizens and permanent residents","Citizens"],answer:3,why:"Article 10(1) says citizens. Article 5 says persons, which is wider. Check who you are dealing with first.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 10(1)"},{id:"qlb2",q:"What does Article 10(2)(a) allow?",options:["The government of the day to impose any restriction it likes","The courts to invent new grounds for restricting speech","State legislatures to restrict speech","Parliament to restrict speech, but only on the grounds the Article names"],answer:3,why:"The grounds are a closed list. And whether a restriction fits one of them is for the court, not for Parliament's own label.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 10(2)(a)"},{id:"qlb3",q:"How do Pung Chen Choon and Sivarasa stand together?",options:["Sivarasa overruled Pung Chen Choon","Pung Chen Choon overruled Sivarasa","Both still stand, and the tension between them is unresolved","Both have been overruled"],answer:2,why:"Writing that one killed off the other is a mistake. Hold both and argue in the alternative.",source:"PP v Pung Chen Choon [1994] 1 MLJ 566 (FC); Sivarasa Rasiah v Badan Peguam Malaysia [2010] 2 MLJ 333 (FC)"},{id:"qlb4",q:"A law never mentions freedom of speech, but in practice it restricts speech. What follows?",options:["It falls outside Article 10 entirely","It is presumed valid","The court looks at what the law does, not at how it is worded","Only the drafter's stated purpose counts"],answer:2,why:"Effect matters more than form. That is what makes the whole analysis worth doing.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 10; a restriction is judged by its effect on the right, not by its wording"},{id:"qlb5",q:"A law clashes with the Constitution. What happens to it?",options:["It is void so far as it clashes","The whole Act dies","It can be set aside later if someone objects","It stays in force until Parliament repeals it"],answer:0,why:"Article 4(1). Only the offending part falls away. Cutting out the bad part is the ordinary course.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 4(1)"}]},{id:"l-emergency-power",moduleId:"m03-constitutional",title:"When the ordinary rules bend: emergency power",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A proclamation of emergency lets the government do things no ordinary law allows. This lesson shows where that power sits, how far it reaches, and which limits stay in place throughout.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 4(1), and the Part of the Federal Constitution dealing with special powers and emergency, cited generally and without an Article number on purpose.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the emergency provisions on purpose. Confirm it in the current Federal Constitution as published by the Attorney General's Chambers. Record the date you checked. It also names no cases. Before relying on the last section, find the Malaysian authority on whether a proclamation of emergency may be reviewed. Check the current time limits on proclamations and on ordinances as well.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"federal-constitution",where:"the Part on special powers and emergency — find it in the table of contents and read the whole Part, including every time limit"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-emergency-power-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"An emergency is a legal state declared under the Constitution, not just a bad week for the country.",h:"A roadblock on a Tuesday morning",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Faizal drives a lorry between Ipoh and Butterworth. On Tuesday he is turned back at a roadblock. A curfew is in force, and he is told to go home."},{t:"p",text:"He asks which Act allows that. The answer may be that no ordinary Act does. A proclamation of emergency was made, and the rules changed."},{t:"p",text:"An emergency in this sense is a legal state. It is declared, it is written down, and it has a beginning and an end. It is not simply a description of a bad situation."},{t:"rule",text:"This Constitution is the supreme law of the Federation and any law passed after Merdeka Day which is inconsistent with this Constitution shall, to the extent of the inconsistency, be void.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 4(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Hold that rule in mind while you read on. Emergency power is the one place where the Constitution loosens its own grip. It does that on its own terms, and the terms are the whole subject."},{t:"caution",text:"This lesson gives no Article number for the emergency provisions, on purpose. That is not shyness. A wrong number here would be copied by every reader who trusted it. Look the Part up yourself in the current text before you write a number down."}]},{id:"sec-l-emergency-power-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A proclamation hands the executive powers that the ordinary constitutional scheme deliberately keeps away from it.",h:"What a proclamation switches on",body:[{t:"p",text:"A proclamation of emergency is made by the Yang di-Pertuan Agong. He acts on the advice of the government of the day."},{t:"p",text:"The trigger is a serious threat. Security, economic life and public order are the recognised grounds. A proclamation may cover the whole country or only one part of it."},{t:"p",text:"Once it is in force, three things become possible that were not possible the day before."},{t:"list",items:["**Parliament may legislate more widely.** It may make laws on subjects it could not normally touch, and laws that would otherwise be inconsistent with the Constitution.","**The Yang di-Pertuan Agong may make ordinances.** Where Parliament is not sitting, he may make emergency ordinances, and an ordinance has the force of an Act.","**The centre may direct a State.** The federal government may give directions on matters that would normally belong to the State alone."]},{t:"p",text:"Read that list again and ask who holds each power. Almost all of it sits on the executive side. Very little of it passes through a court."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Encik Faizal's curfew comes from an emergency ordinance, not from an Act of Parliament. Before you read on — does that make it weaker in law?",options:["Yes — an ordinance is only a rule, so an Act would beat it","No — while the emergency lasts it has the force of an Act","Yes — only a court can restrict a person's movement"],answer:1,reveal:"The second. An emergency ordinance is not a lesser thing than an Act, and that is exactly what makes this power large. If you picked the first, you were reasoning from the ordinary hierarchy of laws, where an instrument made by the executive ranks below a statute. That hierarchy is one of the things a proclamation suspends."}]},{id:"sec-l-emergency-power-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Emergency laws expire with the proclamation, and a short list of subjects stays out of reach throughout.",h:"The limits that stay in place",body:[{t:"p",text:"The power is wide. It is not unlimited, and the limits are the part worth learning precisely."},{t:"list",items:["**Some subjects are out of reach.** A short list of matters cannot be touched even during an emergency. Religion, citizenship and language are on it, along with Malay custom.","**Parliament keeps a say.** The proclamation and any ordinance made under it must be laid before both Houses, and Parliament may annul them.","**Everything expires.** When a proclamation is revoked or lapses, the laws made under it cease to have effect. They do not quietly stay on the books."]},{t:"caution",text:"Each of those three limits carries a time period and a procedure. The periods are short and specific, and they have been amended. Take them from the current text of the Constitution rather than from any summary, including this one."},{t:"p",text:"The third limit does more work than people expect. An emergency law does not become an ordinary law by lasting a long time. It lives and dies with the proclamation that carried it."}]},{id:"sec-l-emergency-power-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Attack the scope and the expiry date, not the government's judgment that a crisis existed.",h:"How far can a court look behind it?",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Faizal wants to challenge the curfew. The useful question is not whether a court can help him. It is which question he should put."},{t:"table",caption:"Four ways to attack an emergency measure, and how much traction each has",columns:["The question","How far a court will go with it"],rows:[["Was there really an emergency?","Not far. The judgment that a threat exists is treated as one for the executive."],["Did the ordinance stay within the proclamation?","Further. A court can read the ordinance against the power that produced it."],["Is the proclamation still in force?","All the way. Revocation and lapse are plain questions of fact and law."],["Does the measure touch an excluded subject?","All the way. That limit is written into the Constitution and a court applies it."]],alt:"Four questions about an emergency measure, and how far a court will go with each. Whether an emergency really existed: not far, because that judgment is treated as the executive's. Whether the ordinance stayed within the proclamation: further, because a court can read the ordinance against the power that produced it. Whether the proclamation is still in force: all the way, since revocation and lapse are plain questions of fact and law. Whether the measure touches an excluded subject: all the way, because the limit is written into the Constitution."},{t:"p",text:"So the strongest arguments are rarely about whether the crisis was genuine. They are about scope, about time, and about the excluded subjects."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"An emergency ordinance is still being enforced two years after the proclamation was revoked. What is the best first argument?",options:["That the ordinance has no force left, because emergency law expires with the proclamation","That the emergency was never genuine in the first place","That the ordinance is unfair to lorry drivers"],answer:0,why:"Expiry is the argument a court can actually decide, and it wins outright. The first answer asks a court to second-guess the executive's judgment about a threat, which is the weakest ground available. The third is not a legal argument at all."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-equality-limits",moduleId:"m03-constitutional",title:"Equality, and the difference the Constitution permits",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"The Constitution guarantees equality before the law, then allows a great deal of difference in how people are treated. This lesson shows how a court sorts a lawful classification from an unlawful one.",source:"Federal Constitution, Part II — the equality guarantee; the Article on the special position of the Malays and the natives of Sabah and Sarawak. The reasonable classification test as applied by the Malaysian courts.",verify:"This lesson states no Article number for the equality guarantee, on purpose. It states none for the special position provisions either. Confirm each in the current Federal Constitution as published by the Attorney General's Chambers. Record the date you checked. It names no cases either. Find the Malaysian authorities on reasonable classification before relying on the two-part test as stated here.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"federal-constitution",where:"Part II in full, then the Article on the special position of the Malays and the natives of Sabah and Sarawak"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-equality-limits-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Unequal treatment and unlawful treatment are two different findings, and the gap between them is the subject.",h:"Two applicants, one licence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Kalsom and Encik Ravi both apply for a hawker licence in the same town. Hers is granted for a stall selling nasi lemak. His is refused."},{t:"p",text:"The council tells him that licences go only to people who have lived in the district for five years. Ravi moved there last year."},{t:"p",text:"Is that unequal treatment? Plainly. Is it unlawful? Not necessarily. The distance between those two answers is what this lesson is about."},{t:"rule",text:"All persons are equal before the law and entitled to the equal protection of the law.",source:"Federal Constitution, Part II — the equality guarantee. This lesson deliberately does not state its Article number; see the verify note."},{t:"p",text:"Notice the word **persons**. This right does not belong to citizens alone. Anybody in the country may rely on it, which is not true of every liberty in Part II."}]},{id:"sec-l-equality-limits-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A real difference between the groups, and a sensible link between that difference and the law's purpose.",h:"Why equality cannot mean treating everyone the same",body:[{t:"p",text:"Law sorts people constantly. Children cannot vote. A doctor needs a licence and a hawker does not. An undischarged bankrupt cannot be a company director."},{t:"p",text:"If equality meant identical treatment, every one of those rules would be void. Nobody thinks that is the law, so the guarantee must mean something narrower."},{t:"p",text:"It does. It forbids treating like cases unalike, and unlike cases alike, without a good reason. Courts test that with a two-part question."},{t:"list",items:["**Is the group real?** The law must sort people by something that actually distinguishes those inside the group from those outside it.","**Does the sorting fit the purpose?** That difference must have a sensible connection to what the law is trying to achieve."]},{t:"p",text:"Lawyers call this the **reasonable classification** test. A classification is just a way of dividing people into groups; a reasonable one is a division the law can justify."},{t:"p",text:"Both halves must be satisfied. A genuine difference that has nothing to do with the law's purpose fails, and so does a purpose served by an imaginary difference."},{t:"example",text:"A scheme pays fuel subsidies only to registered fishermen. The group is real, and the link to a policy about fishing costs is obvious. It stands. A scheme paying subsidies only to people born in January would fail the second half at once."}]},{id:"sec-l-equality-limits-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Concede the obvious difference and put all your weight on the missing link to the law's purpose.",h:"Working the test on Ravi's facts",body:[{t:"predict",prompt:"Ravi is refused because he has lived in the district for under five years. Before you read on — which half of the test is his stronger attack?",options:["The first — length of residence is not a real difference between people","Neither — a licence is a privilege, so equality does not apply to it","The second — the link between years of residence and hawking is weak"],answer:2,reveal:"The second. Length of residence is plainly a real difference, and arguing otherwise burns your credibility on a point you cannot win. All the pressure goes on the connection. Ask what the licensing scheme is actually for. If it is about food hygiene, or about how many stalls a street can hold, five years of residence does no work at all."},{t:"p",text:"That is the ordinary shape of an equality argument. The first half is usually conceded in a sentence. The second half is where the case is won or lost."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not stop at showing that a rule hurts your client. Every rule hurts somebody, and a court knows it. You must show that the sorting itself has no sensible link to what the rule was made to do."}]},{id:"sec-l-equality-limits-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"One part of the Constitution cannot be used to strike down another part of the same Constitution.",h:"Difference the Constitution builds in",body:[{t:"p",text:"Some unequal treatment is not merely tolerated. It is written into the Constitution itself, and three examples come up constantly."},{t:"list",items:["**The special position.** The special position of the Malays and of the natives of Sabah and Sarawak, together with the legitimate interests of other communities, has an Article of its own.","**Personal law.** Marriage and divorce follow different rules for Muslims and non-Muslims. The Constitution's own division of law-making power is what produces that.","**Citizen-only rights.** Some liberties are given to citizens alone. A non-citizen simply cannot claim them, and no equality argument repairs that."]},{t:"p",text:"Where the Constitution itself provides for a difference, the equality guarantee cannot be used to attack it. One part of a document does not strike down another part of the same document."},{t:"caution",text:"The Articles behind those three examples are not numbered here on purpose. This is the area where a wrong Article number does the most damage, because the sentence reads perfectly well either way. Look each one up before you write it down."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A federal Act treats Muslim and non-Muslim spouses differently on divorce. What is the best answer to an equality challenge?",options:["The difference is unconstitutional, because all persons are equal before the law","The Constitution itself provides for separate personal law, so this is a difference it permits","Equality protects citizens only, and both spouses here are citizens"],answer:1,why:"The equality guarantee is read alongside the rest of the Constitution, not against it. The first answer treats the guarantee as absolute, which no court does. The third is wrong twice over: the guarantee speaks of persons, and citizenship would not matter even if it did not."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-religion-of-a-child",moduleId:"m03-constitutional",title:"Who decides a child's religion",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Article 12(4) says the religion of a person under eighteen is decided by a parent or guardian. One word in that clause, read with the Eleventh Schedule, sits at the centre of some of the hardest cases in Malaysian law.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 12(4), the Eleventh Schedule, and Art 121(1A).",verify:"This lesson names no cases on purpose. The Malaysian authority on unilateral conversion of a child has moved more than once. Find the current Federal Court position before relying on the reading given here. Confirm the text of Article 12(4), the Eleventh Schedule and Article 121(1A) in the Federal Constitution as published by the Attorney General's Chambers. Record the date you checked.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"federal-constitution",where:"Article 12 in full, then the Eleventh Schedule, then Article 121(1A)"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-religion-of-a-child-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A single word in Article 12(4) decides whether one parent alone can change a child's religion.",h:"A conversion the other parent knew nothing about",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Suresh and Puan Devi married in Ipoh and have two young children. The marriage breaks down and Suresh moves out."},{t:"p",text:"He converts to Islam. He then takes the two children to a religious office and has them registered as Muslims. Devi learns of it three weeks later."},{t:"p",text:"She wants the registration undone. Her whole argument turns on one word, in one clause, of the Constitution."},{t:"rule",text:"The religion of a person under the age of eighteen years shall be decided by his parent or guardian.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 12(4)"},{t:"p",text:"Read it again, slowly. It says *parent*, in the singular. Suresh says one parent is therefore enough. Devi says it must mean both."},{t:"p",text:"That looks like a squabble about grammar. It is not. Whole families have turned on it."}]},{id:"sec-l-religion-of-a-child-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The Constitution carries its own reading rules, and one of them lets a singular word cover the plural.",h:"The Eleventh Schedule turns one into many",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Constitution does not leave singular and plural to guesswork. It carries its own interpretation rules, and they sit in a Schedule at the back."},{t:"rule",text:"The Eleventh Schedule applies specified interpretation provisions to the Constitution, including the rule that words in the singular include the plural and words in the plural include the singular.",source:"Federal Constitution, Eleventh Schedule"},{t:"p",text:"So *parent* in Article 12(4) can be read as *parents*. That is not a clever lawyer's trick. It is the Constitution's own instruction on how to read itself."},{t:"p",text:"On that reading, the decision belongs to both parents together. A conversion arranged by one of them alone would not satisfy the clause at all."},{t:"caution",text:"The singular-includes-plural rule is a default, not a command. It applies unless the context requires otherwise. So the argument is real and it is strong, but it is not automatic. Expect the other side to argue that the context here does require otherwise."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Suppose the court reads *parent* as *parents*. Before you read on — what happens when the two parents disagree?",options:["The father decides, as the head of the household","The child decides, whatever the parents want","Neither may act alone, so the disagreement has to go to a court"],answer:2,reveal:"Nobody gets to act unilaterally. The clause stops being a power one parent holds and becomes a decision the two must reach together. When they cannot, someone has to resolve it, and that is a court's work. Notice how much a rule about singular and plural just changed."}]},{id:"sec-l-religion-of-a-child-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Frame the question as constitutional compliance, or Article 121(1A) closes the civil court's door.",h:"Why the fight moves to which court",body:[{t:"p",text:"Suppose Devi is right about the words. She still has to find a court willing to say so."},{t:"rule",text:"The courts referred to in Clause (1) shall have no jurisdiction in respect of any matter within the jurisdiction of the Syariah courts.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 121(1A)"},{t:"p",text:"Suresh will say a conversion to Islam is exactly such a matter, so the civil courts must keep out. Devi will say she is asking a different question."},{t:"p",text:"Her question is whether a clause of the Constitution was complied with. That is a constitutional question, and Syariah courts do not decide those."},{t:"p",text:"She has a second point too. She is not a Muslim, so a Syariah court has no jurisdiction over her at all. Sending her there gives her no forum."},{t:"caution",text:"How the question is characterised decides these cases more often than the merits do. State the issue as compliance with Article 12(4), not as the validity of a conversion. The second framing hands the case away before it starts."}]},{id:"sec-l-religion-of-a-child-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Age, then who decided, then whether one parent is enough, then the forum — worked in that order.",h:"The pieces, in the order they come",body:[{t:"table",caption:"Four questions in a child conversion problem, and where each answer comes from",columns:["Step","What you ask","Where the answer comes from"],rows:[["1","Is the child under eighteen?","Article 12(4) applies only below that age."],["2","Who made the decision?","The clause names a parent or guardian, and nobody else."],["3","Does parent mean both parents?","The Eleventh Schedule, unless the context requires otherwise."],["4","Which court hears it?","Article 121(1A), and how the question is characterised."]],alt:"The four questions in order. First, is the child under eighteen, because Article 12(4) applies only below that age. Second, who made the decision, since the clause names a parent or guardian and nobody else. Third, does the word parent mean both parents, which the Eleventh Schedule allows unless the context requires otherwise. Fourth, which court hears it, which turns on Article 121(1A) and on how the question is characterised."},{t:"p",text:"Work them in that order. Most answers start at step four, and starting there concedes the argument on the words without ever making it."},{t:"example",text:"Devi's affidavit opens by stating the children's ages and naming who signed the registration forms. Only then does it reach the Eleventh Schedule. That order is not decoration. It builds the constitutional question the civil court needs before it can take the case at all."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A father converts his fifteen-year-old alone, and the mother objects. What is the strongest opening point?",options:["That the child is old enough to choose for herself","That Article 12(4) says parent, and the Eleventh Schedule lets that be read as both parents","That any conversion to Islam is always a matter for the Syariah courts"],answer:1,why:"Article 12(4) draws its line at eighteen, so the first answer argues against the clause rather than with it. The third is the other side's case, not hers. The second is the point that both fits the words and keeps the matter in a civil court."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-prosecution-discretion",moduleId:"m03-constitutional",title:"Who decides to prosecute, and who may question it",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"The choice to charge somebody, or to stop a charge, belongs to the Attorney General and to nobody else. Article 145(3) says so, and the separation of powers is the reason it is drawn that way.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 145(3); the separation of powers as it operates in Malaysian criminal procedure.",verify:"This lesson names no cases on purpose. Before relying on the second opening in the last section, find the Malaysian authority on reviewing prosecutorial discretion and read it in full. Confirm the text of Article 145(3) in the Federal Constitution as published by the Attorney General's Chambers. Record the date you checked. The lesson also states no section number for the statute making the Attorney General the Public Prosecutor. Look that up before writing it down.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"federal-constitution",where:"Article 145 in full, noticing the clauses around 145(3) that set up the office itself"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-prosecution-discretion-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Starting, running and stopping a prosecution are one power, held by one office, at that office's discretion.",h:"The police report that went nowhere",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Gopal is knocked off his motorcycle on the way to work. His leg is broken and the bike is a write-off. He makes a police report."},{t:"p",text:"The police investigate and send the file on. Six months later Gopal is told there will be no charge. Nobody explains why."},{t:"p",text:"He wants a court to order a prosecution. Before asking whether he can get one, find out who actually holds the decision."},{t:"rule",text:"The Attorney General shall have power, exercisable at his discretion, to institute, conduct or discontinue any proceedings for an offence, other than proceedings before a Syariah court, a native court or a court-martial.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 145(3)"},{t:"p",text:"Three verbs carry that clause. To **institute** is to start proceedings. To **conduct** is to run them. To **discontinue** is to stop them part-way through."},{t:"p",text:"All three sit in one office. And the Article says the power is exercisable at his discretion."}]},{id:"sec-l-prosecution-discretion-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A discretion given by name to one office cannot be exercised by a court on that office's behalf.",h:"What the words at his discretion are doing",body:[{t:"p",text:"Those four words are the heart of the clause. A **discretion** is a choice the law gives you without telling you how to make it."},{t:"p",text:"The Article gives that choice to the Attorney General by name. Three consequences follow, and each of them surprises somebody."},{t:"list",items:["**The police do not decide.** They investigate and hand over a file. A strong file is not a charge.","**A victim cannot start a prosecution as of right.** The offence is against the public, and the public's choice is made by one office.","**A withdrawal is not a mistake.** Discontinuing a charge on the morning of trial is the power being used, not a failure of it."]},{t:"caution",text:"The Attorney General is also the Public Prosecutor, and different statutes use different titles for the same person. Deputy Public Prosecutors act on his behalf. So when a report says the Public Prosecutor has decided something, this is the power being exercised."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Gopal asks the High Court to order that the driver be charged. Before you read on — what is the likely answer?",options:["The court will order the charge if the evidence is strong enough","The court will send the file back to the police to investigate again","The court will not order it, because the choice belongs to the Attorney General"],answer:2,reveal:"The second. The strength of the evidence is not the issue, which is the part that feels wrong. A court that ordered a charge would be exercising a power the Constitution handed to a different branch of government. Gopal is not without a remedy — but it is a civil one, and he has to bring it himself."}]},{id:"sec-l-prosecution-discretion-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Five decisions in a criminal case belong to four different bodies, and most complaints aim at the wrong one.",h:"Separation of powers, in one clause",body:[{t:"p",text:"This is a good place to watch the separation of powers do real work. The phrase names a plain idea."},{t:"p",text:"Parliament makes law. The executive administers and enforces it. The courts decide disputes and say what the law means."},{t:"p",text:"Article 145(3) puts the choice to prosecute firmly on the executive side of that line. A court that refuses to order a charge is not being timid. It is staying on its own side of the line."},{t:"table",caption:"Five decisions in a criminal case, and who owns each one",columns:["The decision","Whose it is"],rows:[["Whether this conduct should be a crime at all","Parliament, when it passes the law"],["Whether to investigate","The police"],["Whether to charge, continue or stop","The Attorney General, under Article 145(3)"],["Whether the charge is proved","The court"],["What sentence follows a conviction","The court, within the range the law sets"]],alt:"Five decisions in a criminal case and who owns each. Whether conduct should be a crime at all belongs to Parliament, when it passes the law. Whether to investigate belongs to the police. Whether to charge, continue or stop belongs to the Attorney General under Article 145(3). Whether the charge is proved belongs to the court. What sentence follows a conviction belongs to the court, within the range the law sets."},{t:"p",text:"Read down that second column. Almost every complaint about a criminal case is really aimed at the wrong row, and naming the right row is half the advice."}]},{id:"sec-l-prosecution-discretion-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The power stops at Syariah, native and court-martial proceedings — and a civil claim was always the victim's own.",h:"The room that is left",body:[{t:"p",text:"The discretion is wide. It is not a wall with no door in it, and two openings are worth knowing."},{t:"list",items:["**It has an edge.** The power covers proceedings for an offence. It does not reach proceedings before a Syariah court, a native court or a court-martial. The Article says so in terms.","**A discretion is given for a purpose.** An exercise of it on a plainly improper basis is arguable. The argument is hard, it rarely succeeds, and it needs authority you have read yourself."]},{t:"p",text:"Gopal has a better route in any case. A criminal charge is not the only way to make somebody answer for a collision."},{t:"example",text:"He sues the driver in the civil courts for negligence. He controls that case himself, and no discretion stands between him and the court. The standard of proof is lower, and the remedy is money for the bike, the hospital bills and the lost wages."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Which of these falls outside the power in Article 145(3)?",options:["A charge of theft in the Magistrates' Court","An appeal by the prosecution against an acquittal","Proceedings before a Syariah court"],answer:2,why:"The Article carves out proceedings before a Syariah court, a native court and a court-martial. Those run under their own systems. The other two are ordinary criminal proceedings and sit squarely inside the power."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-federalism",moduleId:"m03-constitutional",title:"Who may make which law: the federal bargain",minutes:25,summary:"Malaysia has two kinds of law-maker, and each is given its own list of subjects. The Ninth Schedule holds the lists, and Article 75 says what happens when the two collide.",plants:["ms-fed-01","ms-fed-02","ms-fed-03","ms-fed-04","ms-fed-05"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"faruqi-our-constitution",where:"the chapters on the federal structure and the division of powers"}],source:"Federal Constitution Arts 4(1), 74, 75, 76 and the Ninth Schedule (Lists I, II and III).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the current text of the Ninth Schedule — items have been moved and added over time — and check Arts 4(3) and 4(4) and the current Federal Court practice on how a legislative-competence challenge must be brought.",plate:"map",plateCaption:"The Federation and the three lists. Land is a State matter, which is why a federal Land Code needs Article 76 to explain it.",readMinutes:9,sections:[{id:"sec-l-federalism-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Two law-makers share one country, and each may only legislate on its own list.",h:"Two law-makers, one country",body:[{t:"p",text:"Malaysia has two kinds of law-maker. Parliament makes law for the whole country. Each State has its own assembly, which makes law for that State alone."},{t:"p",text:"Neither one can make law about anything it likes. Each is given a list of subjects, and a law outside its own list is invalid however sensible it may be."},{t:"example",text:"Imagine the Selangor assembly passed a law setting the punishment for theft. It would be void. Criminal law belongs to Parliament and not to a State, so every member of the assembly could vote for it and it would still be worth nothing."},{t:"p",text:"Where do the lists come from? The Constitution sets them out, and Article 74 says who may use which of them."},{t:"rule",text:"Parliament may make laws with respect to matters in the Federal List and the Concurrent List; a State Legislature may make laws with respect to matters in the State List and the Concurrent List.",source:"Federal Constitution Arts 74(1) and 74(2), read with the Ninth Schedule"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: Parliament works from the Federal List and the Concurrent List, and a State works from the State List and the Concurrent List. The three lists are printed in the Ninth Schedule to the Constitution."},{t:"p",text:"List I is the Federal List, List II is the State List, and List III is the Concurrent List, which both of them may use. Sabah and Sarawak also have supplementary lists of their own, because they joined the federation on their own terms."}]},{id:"sec-l-federalism-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The Ninth Schedule's three lists — and land is a State matter while criminal law is federal.",anchor:"fig-l-federalism-0",h:"What is on each list",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Ninth Schedule sets out three lists. One says what Parliament may make law about. One says what a State may. The third says what both may. The table is the outline — the Schedule itself runs much longer."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Land is on the State List. So who made the National Land Code, the Act that decides how a lot in Kedah is transferred?",options:["Parliament, with each State adopting it","The Kedah legislature, since land is a State subject","Both together, because land is on the Concurrent List"],answer:0,reveal:"Parliament made it. Article 76 lets Parliament legislate on a State subject so that the law can be uniform. Such a law takes effect in a State when that State adopts it. If you picked the first answer, that is the ordinary reading of the lists, and it is why this one has to be learned as an exception."},{t:"caution",text:"Here is the counter-intuitive one. Land is a State subject, yet the National Land Code is a federal Act. Article 76 is what allows that: it lets Parliament legislate on a State subject so that the law can be uniform across the country. Such a law takes effect in a State only when that State's legislature adopts it. The Code covers the Peninsula, while Sabah and Sarawak have land laws of their own. If you cannot explain that, the land material later will not make sense."},{t:"example",text:"Puan Salmah buys a lot in Kedah. The rules for getting her name onto the title come from a federal Act, the National Land Code. Land is still a State subject. Article 76 is the bridge between those two facts."},{t:"table",caption:"The three lists, in outline",columns:[{label:"List I — Federal"},{label:"List II — State"},{label:"List III — Concurrent"}],rows:[["External affairs, defence, internal security","Islamic law and Malay custom","Social welfare"],["Civil and criminal law and procedure","Land","Town and country planning"],["Administration of justice, citizenship","Agriculture and forestry","Public health"],["Finance, trade and commerce","Local government","Drainage and irrigation"],["Education, medicine and health","State works and water","Protection of wild animals and birds"]],note:"Note the two counter-intuitive entries. **Land** is a State matter, which is why a federal National Land Code needs Art 76 to explain it; **criminal law** is federal, which is why the Penal Code is national.",source:"Federal Constitution, Ninth Schedule"}]},{id:"sec-l-federalism-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Where valid laws collide, federal wins and the State law is void only so far as it clashes.",anchor:"fig-l-federalism-1",h:"When the two clash",body:[{t:"p",text:"Sometimes both law-makers have acted and the two laws do not agree. Encik Rahim needs a licence, and each law sets a different condition for getting one. He cannot obey both of them."},{t:"p",text:"The Constitution settles that contest in a single sentence."},{t:"rule",text:"If any State law is inconsistent with a federal law, the federal law shall prevail and the State law shall, to the extent of the inconsistency, be void.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 75"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the federal law wins. But only the clashing part of the State law dies. The rest of the State law stands, and severing the bad part is the ordinary course."},{t:"p",text:"There is also an earlier question, and it is the one people skip. Article 75 assumes that the federal law is itself a valid one."},{t:"p",text:"A federal Act on a State subject prevails over nothing at all. It is void itself, under Article 4(1)."},{t:"example",text:"A State enactment on the licensing of a trade collides with a federal statute doing the same thing. Do not begin by asking which one wins. Begin by asking whether trade and commerce is a federal subject at all. Only if the federal law is valid does Article 75 do any work."},{t:"steps",title:"The order the questions come in",items:[{h:"Competence first.",text:"Was the subject within the legislature's list at all? A law outside it is void under Art 4(1), and Art 75 never arises."},{h:"Then inconsistency.",text:"If both laws are valid and they collide, Art 75 makes the federal law prevail."},{h:"Then extent.",text:"The State law is void only to the extent of the inconsistency. Severance is the ordinary course, not striking the whole enactment."}]},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A federal Act and a State enactment cover the same ground and contradict each other. Which question comes first?",options:["Whether the federal law was within Parliament's power at all","Which of the two was passed later","Whether the federal law prevails under Article 75"],answer:0,why:"Article 75 assumes the federal law is a valid one. A federal Act on a State subject, with nothing in the Constitution allowing it, is void under Article 4(1). A void law prevails over nothing. Reaching for Article 75 first skips a whole argument."}]},{id:"sec-l-federalism-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The Constitution outranks both, and a later inconsistent law is void to that extent.",h:"The Constitution is the higher law",body:[{t:"p",text:"Why can a court say that a law passed by Parliament is worth nothing? Because one law sits above all the others."},{t:"rule",text:"This Constitution is the supreme law of the Federation and any law passed after Merdeka Day which is inconsistent with this Constitution shall, to the extent of the inconsistency, be void.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 4(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the Constitution beats every other law, and anything inconsistent with it is void so far as it is inconsistent."},{t:"p",text:"In some countries Parliament may pass whatever it likes, and no court may say otherwise. Not here. In Malaysia a court can be asked whether the law-maker had the power at all, and that is the first question about any statute."},{t:"caution",text:"There are rules about *how* you may attack a law on that ground. Articles 4(3) and 4(4) restrict it, and proceedings in the Federal Court need leave — the court's permission to bring the case at all. Check the current text and the current practice before saying how such a challenge must be started. This is a procedural trap rather than a substantive one, and it catches people who have the law right."}]},{id:"sec-l-federalism-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Ask who had power to pass the law before asking whether the law is fair — it often ends there.",h:"Why this question comes first",body:[{t:"p",text:"Suppose a new law limits what people may say at a gathering. The instinct is to ask whether the limit is a fair one, and whether Article 10 permits it."},{t:"p",text:"Stop one step earlier than that. Ask whether the law-maker could pass such a law at all. If it could not, the law is dead, and Article 10 is never reached."},{t:"p",text:"So the order runs: power first, then whether the content is constitutional, then what the words of the provision mean. An answer that goes straight to proportionality has skipped a complete and often decisive argument."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qfd1",q:"Federal law beats State law where the two clash. Which Article says so?",options:["Art 4(1)","Art 74","Art 75","Art 76"],answer:2,why:"Art 75. The State law is void only so far as it clashes, and the rest of it survives.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 75"},{id:"qfd2",q:"Land is a State subject, yet the National Land Code is a federal Act. What makes that possible?",options:["Art 75, because federal law prevails","Art 4(1) supremacy","Art 76, which lets Parliament legislate on State subjects for uniformity","Land is in fact on the Federal List"],answer:2,why:"Art 76 is the uniformity route, and such a law takes effect in a State only when that State adopts it.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 76"},{id:"qfd3",q:"Parliament passes an Act on a subject that is on the State List. What is the position?",options:["It prevails under Art 75","It is valid but unenforceable","It is valid if the State consents afterwards","It is invalid; Art 75 never arises"],answer:3,why:"Art 75 assumes a valid federal law. Power is the earlier question, and Art 4(1) answers it.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 4(1); Ninth Schedule List II"},{id:"qfd4",q:"Which of these is a State subject?",options:["Islamic law and Malay custom","Criminal law and procedure","Citizenship","External affairs"],answer:0,why:"List II holds Islamic law, the personal and family law of Muslims, Malay custom, land and local government.",source:"Federal Constitution, Ninth Schedule List II"},{id:"qfd5",q:"What does Art 4(1) say?",options:["The Constitution is supreme, and inconsistent law is void to that extent","Parliament is sovereign","Federal law prevails over State law","The courts may not review legislation"],answer:0,why:"Supremacy is what makes an argument about legislative power available at all.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 4(1)"},{id:"qfd6",q:"In a fundamental liberties problem, what should you check before asking whether a limit is proportionate?",options:["Whether the plaintiff has standing","Whether the case is reported","Whether the restriction is popular","Whether the law-maker had power to pass the law at all"],answer:3,why:"A limit imposed by a law nobody had power to pass fails before Art 10 is ever reached.",source:"Federal Constitution Arts 4(1), 74 and 10"}]},{id:"l-basic-structure",moduleId:"m03-constitutional",title:"Judicial power, Article 121, and the basic structure argument",minutes:25,summary:"In 1988 the words vesting judicial power in the courts were taken out of Article 121(1). What survived that amendment is still one of the live questions in Malaysian constitutional law.",plants:["ms-jud-01","ms-jud-02","ms-jud-03","ms-jud-04"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"faruqi-document-of-destiny",where:"the treatment of judicial power and the 1988 amendments"}],source:"Federal Constitution Arts 121(1), 121(1A) and 128; the Constitution (Amendment) Act 1988; the Malaysian case law on judicial power and basic structure.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"This is the most unsettled area in this module. Before relying on anything above, find the most recent Federal Court decision on judicial power and on basic structure and read it in full — the direction of authority has reversed before and the case names matter more than the summary.",plate:"pillar-cut",plateCaption:"The 1988 cut across Article 121(1), and the question that has been argued both ways since: what survived it.",readMinutes:6,sections:[{id:"sec-l-basic-structure-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A court's power to decide your case comes from somewhere, and in 1988 that somewhere was rewritten.",h:"What was taken out in 1988",body:[{t:"p",text:"A court's power to decide your case has to come from somewhere. Where it comes from was changed in 1988, and lawyers are still arguing about what that change did."},{t:"p",text:"Before 1988, Article 121(1) provided that the judicial power of the Federation was vested in two High Courts and in the lower courts. Judicial power means the power to decide disputes and to say what the law is."},{t:"p",text:"The amendment replaced that formula. The Article now says the courts shall have such jurisdiction and powers as may be conferred by or under federal law. Jurisdiction means the range of matters a court is allowed to deal with."},{t:"p",text:"On one reading that made the courts' jurisdiction entirely a creature of statute. On another, judicial power is inherent in a constitution that separates the branches, and deleting the sentence that describes it cannot take it away."},{t:"p",text:"Malaysian courts have taken both views."},{t:"diagram",kind:"timeline",title:"What the 1988 amendment did to Article 121(1)",caption:"The argument is about what the replacement left behind. Do not state either reading as settled; find the most recent Federal Court authority before you write about it.",alt:"Three points. Before 1988, Article 121(1) vested the judicial power of the Federation in the two High Courts and the courts below them. In 1988 that formula was replaced, so the courts have such jurisdiction and powers as federal law confers. Since then Malaysian courts have taken both readings: jurisdiction as a creature of statute, and judicial power as inherent in the Constitution.",events:[{year:"pre-1988",label:"Judicial power vested in the courts",note:"Art 121(1), as originally framed"},{year:"1988",label:"The formula is replaced",note:"such jurisdiction as federal law confers"},{year:"since",label:"Both readings taken",note:"creature of statute, or inherent power"}]},{t:"caution",text:"This is a moving area, and that is exactly why it is written about so often. Do not state either reading as settled law. Find the most recent Federal Court authority before you rely on one."}]},{id:"sec-l-basic-structure-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Article 121(1A) takes matters within Syariah jurisdiction away from the civil courts.",h:"Article 121(1A): two systems",body:[{t:"p",text:"Malaysia has civil courts and it has Syariah courts. The Syariah courts are State courts, set up by State law, and they deal with Islamic law matters concerning Muslims."},{t:"p",text:"What happens when one dispute could belong to either system? A clause added in 1988 answers part of that question."},{t:"rule",text:"The courts referred to in Clause (1) shall have no jurisdiction in respect of any matter within the jurisdiction of the Syariah courts.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 121(1A), inserted in 1988"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: where a matter is within Syariah jurisdiction, the civil courts must keep out of it."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Clause (1A) takes a class of matters away from the civil courts. Before you read on — what does it give the Syariah courts?",options:["Nothing — it only removes power from the civil courts","Jurisdiction over Islamic law matters","The final say in any dispute involving a Muslim"],answer:0,reveal:"It gives them nothing. Taking power away from one court is not the same as handing it to another. The Syariah courts get their jurisdiction from State enactments made under List II. That is why the first question in these cases is what the State law actually covers."},{t:"p",text:"Now read the clause again for what it does not do. It is not a grant of power to the Syariah courts. Their own jurisdiction comes from State enactments made under List II."},{t:"p",text:"That distinction matters. If the matter is outside Syariah jurisdiction, clause (1A) does nothing at all, and the civil court keeps its power."},{t:"example",text:"A man dies and his family disagree about who should bury him, and by what rites. Or a marriage ends, one parent converts, and both go to court over a child. One dispute can have a civil side and a Syariah side. These are the hardest cases, and they usually turn on how the question is characterised rather than on any disagreement about the rule."}]},{id:"sec-l-basic-structure-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Could Parliament amend away judicial review itself? That is the basic structure argument.",h:"The basic structure argument",body:[{t:"p",text:"Parliament can amend the Constitution. Suppose it amended away the courts' power to review anything at all. Could it?"},{t:"p",text:"The basic structure argument says no. Certain features are so fundamental that they cannot be removed even by an amendment passed in the proper way. Judicial power, the rule of law and the separation of powers are the usual candidates."},{t:"p",text:"The idea began in India. It has been received in Malaysia unevenly."},{t:"caution",text:"The Malaysian position has changed direction more than once. There are Federal Court decisions rejecting the doctrine and later ones applying it. If you write about this, write about the sequence and its current state, and give the citations. An assertion here with no case name is worth nothing."},{t:"p",text:"The practical use of the doctrine is narrow. It is an argument of last resort against a constitutional amendment, and not a general licence to strike down ordinary legislation. Confusing those two is the commonest error made with it."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"What does the basic structure argument claim?",options:["That the Constitution cannot be amended","That some features cannot be removed, even by a properly passed amendment","That a law inconsistent with the Constitution is void"],answer:1,why:"It is an argument about the limits of the power to amend. The last answer is Article 4(1), which is settled and is a different point. Treating the doctrine as a general licence to strike down ordinary legislation is the commonest error made with it."}]},{id:"sec-l-basic-structure-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Constitutional questions have their own forum, and getting it wrong is not a small mistake.",h:"Where a constitutional question goes",body:[{t:"p",text:"Forum means which court hears the matter. On constitutional questions the Constitution is specific about it."},{t:"list",items:["Only the Federal Court may determine whether a law made by Parliament or a State Legislature is invalid because it relates to a matter outside legislative competence.","The Federal Court also determines disputes between two States, or between the Federation and a State.","A constitutional question arising in the High Court may be referred up to the Federal Court. The Constitution and the Courts of Judicature Act 1964 set out the mechanism and its limits."]},{t:"p",text:"Getting the forum wrong wastes the case. An answer that reaches the right constitutional conclusion in the wrong court has not advised the client at all."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Which court hears a constitutional question",caption:"Forum is decided by the kind of question, not by where the dispute started. A High Court cannot pronounce a statute invalid on the ground that it was beyond legislative power.",alt:"A question about the kind of constitutional question, branching three ways. Whether a law made by Parliament or a State Legislature is invalid: only the Federal Court may determine it. A dispute between two States, or between the Federation and a State: the Federal Court. A constitutional question arising in the High Court: it may be referred up to the Federal Court.",question:"What kind of constitutional question is it?",branches:[{cond:"is a law invalid?",label:"Federal Court only",note:"beyond legislative power"},{cond:"State v State, or Federation v State",label:"Federal Court",note:"disputes between governments"},{cond:"arising in the High Court",label:"May be referred up",note:"to the Federal Court"}]}]}],quiz:[{id:"qbs1",q:"What did the 1988 amendment do to Art 121(1)?",options:["Vested judicial power in Parliament","Replaced the vesting of judicial power with jurisdiction conferred by federal law","Abolished appeals to the Privy Council","Created the Court of Appeal"],answer:1,why:"The vesting words went out and a conferral formula came in. What that did is still argued about.",source:"Constitution (Amendment) Act 1988; Federal Constitution Art 121(1)"},{id:"qbs2",q:"What does Art 121(1A) do?",options:["Grants jurisdiction to the Syariah courts","Makes Syariah courts superior courts","Creates a right of appeal to the Federal Court","Removes civil court jurisdiction over matters within Syariah jurisdiction"],answer:3,why:"It subtracts jurisdiction from the civil courts. It grants the Syariah courts nothing; their power comes from State enactments.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 121(1A)"},{id:"qbs3",q:"A matter falls outside Syariah jurisdiction. What follows for Art 121(1A)?",options:["It does not apply and the civil court retains jurisdiction","The civil court still has no jurisdiction","The matter must be transferred","The Federal Court decides"],answer:0,why:"Clause (1A) only bites where the matter is within Syariah jurisdiction in the first place.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 121(1A)"},{id:"qbs4",q:"What is the basic structure doctrine, at its narrowest?",options:["That all legislation must be proportionate","That the Federal Court may overrule itself","That some fundamental features cannot be removed even by a constitutional amendment","That State law yields to federal law"],answer:2,why:"It is a limit on the power to amend, not a general power of review over ordinary laws.",source:"The Malaysian case law on judicial power and basic structure"},{id:"qbs5",q:"Which court decides whether a law is invalid for want of legislative competence?",options:["The Federal Court, exclusively","The High Court","The Court of Appeal","Any court seised of the matter"],answer:0,why:"Article 128 gives the Federal Court exclusive jurisdiction over that question.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 128"}]}];export{e as default};
